      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright Â© 2016 American Society for Bariatric Surgery. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "After Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, calcium and vitamin D deficiencies are frequently reported. In the presence of adequate vitamin D levels, calcium deficiency is caused by a lower efficacy of the intestinal calcium transport."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To investigate whether the use of a simple clinical score quantifying bowel habits (fecal score [FS]) correlates with the degree of secondary hyperparathyroidism that arises to compensate for calcium deficiency postsurgery."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Large peripheral hospital."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Seventy-five patients supplemented with calcium and vitamin D were prospectively studied before and 6 and 12 months after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. FS, calcium (mmol/L), phosphate (mmol/L), magnesium (mmol/L), vitamin D (nmol/L), and parathyroid hormone (PTH; pmol/L) were measured in each patient."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Mean body mass index was 44.7±5.4 kg/m(2) preoperatively and decreased to 34.3±5.0 kg/m(2) at 6 months and 30.8±4.8 kg/m(2) at 12 months, corresponding to a total weight loss of 23.2±5.9% and 30.9±8.3% respectively. There were no significant changes in serum calcium levels. Mean PTH levels rose from 3.5 pmol/L at baseline to 4.1 pmol/L at 6 months (P = .01) and to 4.9 pmol/L at 12 months (P<.001). Nine patients (12%) had increased PTH levels at 6 months, and 14 patients (19%) had increased PTH levels at 12 months. A significant positive correlation between FS and PTH at 12 months was found, which persisted after adjusting for vitamin D levels."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "FS is positively correlated with secondary hyperparathyroidism using vitamin D-adjusted PTH levels as a biochemical marker. The present study in humans confirms the relation reported in animal studies. These results emphasize that managing stool habits are important after bariatric surgery."
          }
        ]
      },
